WEBVTT

00:04.120 --> 00:04.440
All right.

00:04.480 --> 00:06.440
So now let's talk about the composite index.

00:06.440 --> 00:11.520
It is an index that has multiple columns inside the same index.

00:11.520 --> 00:14.760
So far we have used only one column in the index.

00:14.960 --> 00:17.280
But we can go and specify multiple columns.

00:17.280 --> 00:23.640
And that's because sometimes our rare conditions are complicated and based on multiple columns.

00:23.640 --> 00:29.360
So for example let's say that we are searching for country equal to USA.

00:29.640 --> 00:34.800
And at the same time we are saying the score should be higher than 500.

00:34.800 --> 00:38.040
So that means in this condition we are using two columns.

00:38.040 --> 00:40.080
And we would like to speed up this query.

00:40.080 --> 00:41.080
So how are we going to do it.

00:41.080 --> 00:47.600
So we're going to go and create let's say an index and give it a name DB customers.

00:47.760 --> 00:55.000
And let's say country score on sales DB customers.

00:55.480 --> 00:58.680
And now it is very important to do the following thing.

00:58.880 --> 01:04.600
Now we have to go and define a list of columns that you want to be included in this index.

01:04.600 --> 01:10.880
And it is very crucial and important that you get the same order as your query.

01:10.880 --> 01:14.820
So your query starts with the country and then the score.

01:14.980 --> 01:17.220
You have to do it the same thing in the index.

01:17.580 --> 01:21.580
So the first column is going to be the country and then the score.

01:21.620 --> 01:24.980
So it must be the same order as your query.

01:24.980 --> 01:28.100
So let's go and create this index.

01:28.100 --> 01:33.060
And if you go to the indexes over here you can see that we have created our new index.

01:33.060 --> 01:38.500
So now once you create such an index and your table is going to be like always updating this index you

01:38.500 --> 01:40.900
have to be committed and responsible.

01:40.900 --> 01:45.740
So in your queries, if you want to filter the data using country and score, always start with the

01:45.740 --> 01:51.340
country, then the score in order to be able to use the index optimizer.

01:51.340 --> 01:54.780
So if you do it like this, the index is going to be working.

01:54.780 --> 01:57.860
But if you go and query the way around.

01:57.860 --> 02:04.620
So you start with the score and then the country, the SQL will not be using your index.

02:04.620 --> 02:11.740
So either you adjust your queries or you have to go and recreate the index based on this switch.

02:11.740 --> 02:14.300
So be very careful with the composite indexes.

02:14.420 --> 02:16.340
The order is very critical.

02:16.700 --> 02:19.940
So you're going to have it exactly like the query.

02:19.940 --> 02:21.060
And now you might say you know what.

02:21.100 --> 02:24.060
Now we have like a nice index for those two columns.

02:24.300 --> 02:30.520
What can happen if I go and use in my query only one of them, like for example, the country.

02:30.520 --> 02:37.400
So now the question is if I go and execute this query is the SQL is using this index even though that

02:37.400 --> 02:38.640
I don't have the score?

02:38.880 --> 02:43.880
Well, yes, because it follows the leftmost prefix rule.

02:43.880 --> 02:49.920
So what this means SQL can use the index if you are using always the left columns.

02:49.920 --> 02:52.840
So here in our index country is on the left.

02:52.880 --> 02:54.920
That's why it is working over here.

02:54.920 --> 02:58.840
But if you go and skip the left column it will not work.

02:58.840 --> 03:07.080
So if you go over here, for example, and say let's go and select only the score and it is like higher

03:07.560 --> 03:14.240
than 500, what we have done, we have skipped the country in this query, and that's why it will not

03:14.240 --> 03:14.800
be working.

03:14.800 --> 03:20.880
So as long as you are including the left columns, it will work even though it is only one column.

03:20.880 --> 03:26.520
So in this scenario the first query can use the index, the second one will not be using it.

03:26.560 --> 03:30.080
So now let me give you a very simple example in order to understand how this works.

03:30.080 --> 03:34.160
So let's say that we have an index using four columns a, b, C, d.

03:34.200 --> 03:38.940
Now in your query, if you go and target the column A, the index is going can be used.

03:38.980 --> 03:42.340
Now, the same thing can happen if you go and use A and B.

03:42.500 --> 03:46.060
So if you are using those two columns you will be using the index.

03:46.180 --> 03:51.180
So those are where the index will be used.

03:51.300 --> 03:56.540
So now let's have the scenarios where the index wants to be used.

03:56.660 --> 04:02.940
So for example if you go and just jump immediately to the column B so you are not using the left column

04:02.980 --> 04:03.420
the A.

04:03.620 --> 04:05.900
That's why you will not be using the index.

04:05.900 --> 04:12.300
And as well in your query, if you are using a and you are skipping the B, so you have a and then C,

04:12.700 --> 04:14.260
you will not be using the index.

04:14.340 --> 04:17.380
So you have always to use always the left columns.

04:17.500 --> 04:22.020
So here if you are using a b, c you will be using the index.

04:22.060 --> 04:23.980
And let's see here you are using a b.

04:24.020 --> 04:26.220
And then you jump and skip to the d.

04:26.260 --> 04:27.740
You will not be using the index.

04:27.740 --> 04:30.580
So this is what we mean with the leftmost prefix rule.

04:30.980 --> 04:33.020
By using the composite index.

04:33.140 --> 04:38.180
So if you are using multiple columns inside one index, be careful with the order of the columns that

04:38.180 --> 04:39.220
you are defining.

04:39.340 --> 04:39.740
All right.

04:39.740 --> 04:43.420
So that's all for this category clustered and non-clustered index.

04:43.540 --> 04:49.140
Now we're going to move to the second category where we talk about the indexes by the storage the row

04:49.180 --> 04:50.940
store and the column store.
